OBJ: the word buffer must not be a fixed 4096-byte member (and must stop at EOF)

Backport of upstream assimp commit 0404c875 ("Migrate OBJ parser from
fixed-size stack buffer to std::string", PR #6714) -- merged after our pinned
release tag v6.0.5. No CVE, but it is the same defect family as the four FBX
ones and .obj is a first-class Jahshaka import format, so it rides along.

The bug
-------
`ObjFileParser::copyNextWord(char *pBuffer, size_t length)` copied a
whitespace-delimited token into the class's fixed `char mBuffer[4096]`,
bounded only by a length check that was one `ai_assert` away from being the
whole guard -- and `ai_assert` compiles to nothing without ASSIMP_BUILD_DEBUG.
Worse, the line-continuation branch (`\`) advanced the iterator twice with no
end check, so a file ending in a backslash walked past the end of the buffer.

The fix
-------
`mBuffer` becomes a std::string that grows to the token; copyNextWord takes no
arguments; every caller reads it through `c_str()`; and each iterator advance
in the continuation branch is followed by an end-of-buffer test.

Documented deviations from upstream, all cosmetic:
  * upstream's new early-return guards are indented with a mix of tabs and
    "space before tab"; normalised to the file's four-space style,
  * upstream's CMakeLists.txt hunk (a global `-Wunsafe-buffer-usage` for every
    Clang build of assimp) is dropped -- it is not part of the fix and would
    add thousands of warnings to the macOS build,
  * upstream's test/unit/utObjTools.cpp hunk is dropped (ASSIMP_BUILD_TESTS is
    OFF in irisgl/CMakeLists.txt, so that file is never compiled),
  * upstream also deletes the trailing newline of ObjFileParser.h and leaves a
    dangling empty `private:` label; neither is reproduced here.

